> Corticosteroids are used because they are **broad, powerful anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive agents**:
> - they induce **annexin-1 (lipocortin)**, which inhibits **phospholipase A₂** and so blocks the release of arachidonic acid — suppressing **both** the prostaglandin (COX) and the leukotriene (lipoxygenase) pathways, unlike NSAIDs;
> - they **suppress transcription of inflammatory genes** (COX-2, iNOS, IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, TNF-α) through the glucocorticoid receptor and inhibition of NF-κB and AP-1;
> - they **reduce the number and activity of inflammatory cells** — lymphopenia, eosinopenia, reduced monocyte and macrophage function, decreased capillary permeability and oedema;
> - they **relieve pain, swelling, stiffness and systemic symptoms rapidly** and can halt a severe flare while slower-acting disease-modifying drugs take effect;
> - they can be given **systemically or locally (intra-articular)** to limit systemic toxicity.
>
> Long-term use is limited by **adrenal suppression, osteoporosis, infection, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, peptic ulceration, cataract, cushingoid features and growth retardation**.
